    {
      "question": "q-50136",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "042f38bc06d6bfe2bcf70c37ada073b0c9e25cffbb8b9819d8ae1ef2ad013c0d",
      "new_sha256": "2adac0745dffe43017deb3b3e435878a8e7b67a31b7008d9f2969a2d6f1d6f75",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "q-50136",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{2x}-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}y & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/state.json",
          "file_sha256": "cf8cf88632a9c5ad74cf8897a740692aa2381666de5c0f8e315ed6b3974fb887"
        }
      ],
