    {
      "question": "q-50139",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "d83bc84c81eceb66707826a08803d8a4b8296b77902e1cd9c8fd9f3d6668c689",
      "new_sha256": "98aa8c0006e753ef2ca05f02585ae4ab990b77afae2bc6afdfd7c15b8ac0444e",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "q-50139",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-10\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-10x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-10\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D \\\\  & =-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/state.json",
          "file_sha256": "cf8cf88632a9c5ad74cf8897a740692aa2381666de5c0f8e315ed6b3974fb887"
